From c78c521850f7bc8f9a38339e2ed84049a6583e24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Miroshnichenko <serg.zorg@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 05:31:05 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] :input_paste reads file lists and acts on the files
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A file manager doesn't put text on the clipboard when files are copied:
it puts a text/uri-list, and GNOME and KDE also an
x-special/gnome-copied-files list of file:// URIs. terminal-clipboard
only asks for UTF-8 text, so ctrl-v either inserted the raw URIs into
the input — a pattern which matches nothing — or inserted nothing.

The clipboard module asks for the file-list types first and decodes them
into local paths, and input_paste now distinguishes the two payloads:

- with an empty input in a tree, a single directory is entered, a single
  file is revealed (or its directory opened, when it is outside the
  tree), and several files open the directory that holds them;
- with something typed, or for plain text, the paste still inserts text,
  but a file list is inserted as decoded paths, so `:show ` followed by
  ctrl-v inserts a path the verb can resolve.

The jump is built as a normal verb invocation (`:focus` or `:show`), so
it is parsed, resolved and drawn like the same command typed by hand.
